<article class="env-proposition" id="04LY">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04LY">Proposition <span data-tag="04LY">9.6.2.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $W$ be a collection of morphisms of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Suppose that every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ of $W$ admits a relative codiagonal $\gamma _{C/D}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$ which also belongs to $W$ (Variant <a data-tag="04K7" href="/tag/04K7">7.6.2.23</a>). Then an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is $W$-local if and only if it is weakly $W$-local. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Fix an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $h_{X}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$ denote the functor represented by $X$. We wish to show that, for every morphism $w: C \rightarrow D$ which belongs to $W$, the image $h_{X}(w)$ is a homotopy equivalence of Kan complexes. By virtue of Remark <a data-tag="04HJ" href="/tag/04HJ">3.5.1.19</a>, it will suffice to show that $h_{X}(w)$ is $n$-connective for every integer $n \geq 0$. The proof proceeds by induction on $n$. In the case $n = 0$, we wish to show that the composition map $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(D,X) \rightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}(C,X)$ is surjective on connected components, which follows from our assumption that $X$ is weakly $W$-local. Let us therefore assume that $n &gt; 0$. Using the criterion of Corollary <a data-tag="04HA" href="/tag/04HA">3.5.1.29</a> (together with Exercise <a data-tag="04K8" href="/tag/04K8">7.6.3.13</a>), we are reduced to proving the $(n-1)$-connectivity of the relative diagonal of $h_{X}(w)$ (formed in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$). Since the functor $h_{X}$ preserves limits (Proposition <a data-tag="039L" href="/tag/039L">7.4.1.22</a>), we can identify the relative diagonal of $h_{X}(w)$ with $h_{X}( \gamma _{C/D} )$, where $\gamma _{C/D}: D \coprod _{C} D \rightarrow D$ denotes a relative codiagonal of $w$. By assumption, we can arrange that $\gamma _{C/D}$ is also contained in $W$, so the desired result follows from our inductive hypothesis. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
